
	    // asserts
            Assert.True(New_arrListLastIndexOfX==-1);

        }
    }			
}
```

After running these, you should see something like the picture below: 

![Demonstration on Pex](./pex_demo.png)

In these tests, we covered the `Add` and `Remove` operations of an `ArrayList`. For each operation, we made sure to keep track of the old and new state of the list before and after the operation. In a `Put_AddContract` testmethod we validate the `Add` method by verifying the Count, IndexOf and LastIndexOf of the item inserted to see if they match expected states. For a `Put_RemoveContract` test method, we not only validate the Count, IndexOf and LastIndexOf to see if they match expected states, but also verify that we do not find the item after it has been removed from the list.

The Pex plugin also provides a GUI for you to visualize and interact with the generated tests. The image is a visualization of the tests generated by the `PUT_RemoveContract` method. There you can see all the generated input and outcome, which makes it easier to debug your tests, especially in scenarios with many parameters or conditions.

Remember that the picture above is just a static visualization snapshot; you have to run the tests again to see a dynamic one, with your actual parameters substituted and the outcome in place.

As in any other testing tool, the more diverse and complex the codebase, the more tests you should write and how comprehensive these are, as well as how diverse they should be. 

This gives you a good starting point. Remember to always think about the possible edge cases and unexpected inputs that might occur in your system when doing the testing, as this can save you a lot of debugging time later. 
