    public string Y { get; set; }
}

The error message states that it is unable to compare two different objects of type MyClass, even though I'm creating them with new() {}

So the error is here, because the framework cannot find a suitable method for the comparison of objects. This makes sense, since the objects have no meaningful way to be equal or not equal.

However, I'm not sure on how to solve it. Would you suggest something here?


A: The issue you are encountering is due to object comparison in Pex and it's not designed to properly compare objects. For complex object types, Pex relies on the type to have a default constructor which creates an equivalent object of the same type. It cannot test complex types with properties or without default constructors.

For your use case, you can consider using the PexInstrumentedMembers or the PexInstrumentedBases attribute on your test class or the test method. This technique will force Pex to instrument your member variables and call through constructors. 

This will allow Pex to use the class-level equality method (if any), or delegate comparison to framework-specific or third-party code.

Please find the adjusted code below, which should resolve your issue:

```
[TestFixture, PexInstrumentedMembers]
public class PexStressTest
{
    [PexMethod, PexInstrumentedBases] // <--- added attributes
    public void TestMethod3( [PexAssumeUnderTest] MyClass[] arr) 
    { 
        AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(true); // to run the test 
        arr[2] = arr[3]; // to generate different paths 
        arr[1] = arr[3]; // to generate different paths 
    }
    ...
}
```
This approach should allow Pex to properly infer the object equality and other comparison semantics for your class. 

As one more option, there's the InstrumentedForCodeCoverage attribute which you can use instead (but remember that this instrumentation would also add overhead on the runtime).

Note that using Pex on complex object types might be harder to keep up with as the object and its properties evolve. Be sure to follow best practices for maintaining your tests to ensure they stay robust and maintain a high level of code coverage.
 
Hope this helps.

Note: You are responsible for verifying that these solutions meet your application's requirements. It's always good to verify the results in a controlled, known environment before using them in production.
 
Good luck with your testing!! Please let us know if you have questions.


