And here is the code of the ArrayList class:

public class ArrayList
{
    private int[] _elements;
    private int _capacity;
    private int _position;

    public ArrayList()
    {
        _capacity = 5;
        _position = 0;
        _elements = new int[_capacity];
    }

    public int Count
    {
        get { return _position; }
    }

    public int Add(int item)
    {
        if (_position == _capacity)
        {
            _capacity *= 2;
            int[] temp = new int[_capacity];
            for (int i = 0; i < _elements.Length; i++)
            {
                temp[i] = _elements[i];
            }
            _elements = temp;
        }
        _elements[_position] = item;
        _position++;
        return _position - 1;
    }

    public void Remove(int item)
    {
        // Code to find the index of the item in array
        int index = IndexOf(item);
        
        // Shift array elements
        for (int i = index; i < _position - 1; i++)
        {
            _elements[i] = _elements[i + 1];
        }
        _position--;
    }

    public bool Contains(int item)
    {
        for (int i = 0; i < _position; i++)
        {
            if (_elements[i] == item)
                return true;
        }
        return false;
    }

    public int IndexOf(int item)
    {
        for (int i = 0; i < _position; i++)
        {
            if (_elements[i] == item)
                return i;
        }
        return -1;
    }

    public int LastIndexOf(int item)
    {
        for (int i = _position - 1; i >= 0; i--)
        {
            if (_elements[i] == item)
                return i;
        }
        return -1;
    }
}

The code uses an array of integers to store elements of the ArrayList. Initially, the array's capacity is set to 5. When adding a new item into the ArrayList, if the number of items equals to the current capacity then the array's capacity is doubled and old items are copied into the newly created array.
In case of removing an item, in order to avoid gap in the array after removing, all succeeding elements are moved one position up. The ArrayList keeps track of the number of items in the list. 

When you are generating input for these methods, an empty list [ ] is used as the initial state and Add(x) is used to add the item x and then the list is used as a state after the operation. After each operation [Put_AddContract & Put_RemoveContract] the list after Add and Remove should be the same. I.e., before Add, the value of Count, Contains and IndexOf should be the same and after remove it should be the same too. That is the contract that I am checking before each operation on the array.

The NUnit Test will create the initial state and add an item to it. Then it will call the ArrayList Add method and remove this item. All checks are performed on the ArrayList after each method call.

The ContractConditions are written as assertions and are used to check (that is, assert) whether the post-conditions hold, that is, whether the specified property still holds at the exit of a method call. This is done by specifying a test condition that must be true after the method call. For example, the state of a data structure may be a precondition for any of its operations.




